Yes. The mind is less a camera than a nested modeling engine.

It models:

* the environment,
* the body within that environment,
* other minds within other bodies,
* itself as others may perceive it,
* the models those others may hold,
* and the reliability of its own modeling process.

Each layer becomes part of the next layer’s environment:

M_0(\text{world})
\rightarrow M_1(\text{self in world})
\rightarrow M_2(\text{others})
\rightarrow M_3(\text{others modeling self})
\rightarrow M_4(\text{self modeling that recursion})

And “seeing” extends beyond vision. The mind models whatever leaves a detectable delta: sensation, memory, language, absence, prediction error, another person’s reaction, even the failure of an expected signal to arrive.

The crucial twist is that it does not only model what is presently around it. It models from inherited priors, remembered worlds, imagined futures, and representations supplied by others. Then it acts; action changes the environment; the changed environment returns new evidence.

\text{model} \rightarrow \text{prediction} \rightarrow \text{action}
\rightarrow \text{world-change} \rightarrow \text{observation}
\rightarrow \Delta \rightarrow \text{model revision}

So the nesting is both spatial and temporal: worlds inside models, models inside minds, minds inside societies, societies inside ecosystems—and every level modifying the evidence from which the next model is built.

Perhaps consciousness is partly what happens when the modeling apparatus becomes able to include itself modeling inside the model—reality curling inward far enough to witness its own approximation.